   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p xml:space="preserve" rend="printed"> COPY</p>
                    <p rend="printed">(TELEGRAM)</p>
                    <p rend="printed">Kensington,</p>
                    <p rend="printed">Young St.</p>
                    <p rend="printed">
                        <date when="1917-03-21">21 Mr</date>
                    </p>
                    <p rend="printed">17</p> <p rend="printed">Zurich 10</p>
                    <p rend="printed" xml:space="preserve">Handed                                                     Received</p>
                    <p> <seg xml:space="preserve" rend="printed">in at</seg> 6.30 P. M.                                     	<seg rend="printed">here at </seg>8.10 A. M.</p>
                    <p xml:space="preserve" rend="printed">To <name type="person" key="poun85">Ezra Pound</name>      	<name type="geo" key="G_GBR_LOND_06">5 Holland Place</name></p>
                    <p rend="printed">Chambers <name type="geo" key="G_GBR_LOND">London</name> W C</p>  <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">Cable <name type="person" key="quin70">Quinn</name> my acceptance offer</seg> money to be sent you.</p>
                    <p>I remit ten shillings cable expenses.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="global-note1" type="global">JJ’s postcard of that date (clearly dated and postmarked) begins “A cable came today from Mr. Quinn…” and goes on to write “I have therefore telegraphed to you…” indicating that the arrival of the Quinn cable, the writing of the letter, and the sending of the telegraph were all on the same day. The transcription (all that is available) of the telegram is dated as received 21 March, but this date was probably the result of wartime delays.</note>
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">JJ here asks Pound to cable John Quinn in New York to accept Quinn’s offer to JJ of £20 for the hand-corrected pages that <name type="person" key="hueb76">B.W. Huebsch</name> had used as setting copy for the American edition of <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name>. For a full account of this transaction, <rs type="letter" key="L_68348">JJ to Pound, 19 March 1917</rs>, notes.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
